.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-2-Clause
.\"
.\" swapd(8) -- NextBSD's dynamic pager (nextbsd/nextbsd-userland#178).
.\" A NextBSD-original page; the daemon is modelled on Darwin's
.\" dynamic_pager(8), whose options it keeps.
.Dd November 1, 2026
.Dt SWAPD 8
.Os NextBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm swapd
.Nd grow and reclaim swap files under memory pressure
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl F Ar filename
.Op Fl S Ar filesize
.Op Fl H Ar high-water-trigger
.Op Fl L Ar low-water-trigger
.Op Fl P Ar priority
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
manages a pool of swap files, adding one when the kernel runs short of swap
and removing one when it is no longer needed.
It is NextBSD's form of Darwin's
.Nm dynamic_pager ,
and it is started by
.Xr launchd 8
from
.Pa /System/Library/LaunchDaemons/org.nextbsd.swapd.plist .
.Pp
.Nm
registers a Mach port with the kernel through
.Fn macx_triggers .
The kernel sends that port one
.Fn default_pager_space_alert
per registration: HI_WAT_ALERT when free swap falls below
.Va vm.nswap_lowat ,
and LO_WAT_ALERT when it rises back above
.Va vm.nswap_hiwat .
.Nm
registers again after every alert.
.Pp
On HI_WAT_ALERT
.Nm
turns on the preallocated spare file if there is one, otherwise it creates the
next file
.Ar filename Ns Ar N
and turns it on with
.Xr swapon 2 .
Files are preallocated, never sparse.
The first two are 256 MiB, then each size doubles up to 1 GiB, and no file is
larger than physical memory.
A file is not created if it would take more than one eighth of the free space
on the volume, or if total swap would pass half of
.Va vm.swap_maxpages
(all of it once
.Va kern.maxswzone
has been raised).
After a file is added,
.Nm
preallocates one spare file, so that the next addition is only a
.Xr swapon 2 .
.Pp
While more than one file is in use,
.Nm
checks free swap every minute.
The newest file is removed only if free swap would stay above
.Va vm.nswap_hiwat
without it, and that has held for five minutes.
It is turned off with
.Xr swapoff 2
without
.Dv SWAPOFF_FORCE ;
if that fails with
.Er ENOMEM
the file is kept and tried again later.
The removed file becomes the spare.
The oldest file in use is never removed.
.Pp
At start
.Nm
turns on
.Ar filename Ns 0
if it exists and removes the other files, as
.Nm dynamic_pager
empties its directory.
Files that are still in use for swap, after
.Nm
was restarted, are kept.
If
.Pa /etc/fstab
lists swap,
.Nm
runs
.Ql swapon -a
once.
.Pp
Swap files need UFS.
If the directory is on another file system, such as ZFS,
.Nm
logs that and exits with status 0.
.Pp
The options are those of
.Nm dynamic_pager :
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Fl F Ar filename
The base name of the swap files.
The default is
.Pa /private/var/vm/swapfile .
.It Fl S Ar filesize
Use files of this fixed size, in bytes, instead of the growing sizes.
.It Fl H Ar high-water-trigger
Add a file when less than this many bytes of swap are free.
This sets
.Va vm.nswap_lowat .
The default leaves the kernel's threshold, about 10% of the configured swap.
.It Fl L Ar low-water-trigger
Consider removing a file when more than this many bytes of swap are free.
This sets
.Va vm.nswap_hiwat .
The default leaves the kernel's threshold, about 25% of the configured swap.
.It Fl P Ar priority
Accepted and ignored, as in
.Nm dynamic_pager .
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/var/log/swapd.stderr" -compact
.It Pa /private/var/vm/swapfile*
swap files
.It Pa /var/log/swapd.stderr
log
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr swapoff 2 ,
.Xr swapon 2 ,
.Xr launchd 8 ,
.Xr swapinfo 8
.Sh HISTORY
.Nm
first appeared in NextBSD.
Darwin's
.Nm dynamic_pager
first appeared in Mac OS X.
